@pOpO1-9 I have to walk part of that back. I re-ran the branch at cf3e5fe: the 7 new tests pass, but 7 existing tests in `test_derender.py` now fail (`test_e2e_plain_roundtrip`, `test_e2e_token_identity`, `test_e2e_parsed_reasoning`, ...). Example: `'The answer is four' == 'The answer is four.'`. Those tests encode text into token ids with no trailing stop token and default `finish_reason="stop"`, so the unconditional drop eats a real token. `test_e2e_token_identity` is the RL invariant, `encode(derender(ids)) == ids`, so this is a contract, not just a fixture.

Real engine output does end with the stop/EOS id (the detokenizer re-appends it), so the rule is right on real traffic, but derender shouldn't assume that about its input. The membership check you had was correct; it just needed the full set:

- drop the last token only if it is in the client's `stop_token_ids`, equals `tokenizer.eos_token_id`, or is in the model's generation-config `eos_token_id` list (`model_config.try_get_generation_config()`), which the engine merges into `stop_token_ids` server-side;
- `stop_reason` is `None` when it is the primary EOS (engine does the same in `check_stop`), otherwise the token id;
- do the token check before the string scan, on the remaining text, which is the engine's order.

Sorry for the detour.

